#: ../../../tutorial.rst:8
msgid "Tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:10
msgid ""
"xonsh is a shell language and command prompt. Unlike other shells, xonsh is "
"based on Python, with additional syntax added that makes calling subprocess "
"commands, manipulating the environment, and dealing with the file system "
"easy.  The xonsh command prompt gives users interactive access to the xonsh "
"language."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:16
msgid ""
"While all Python code is also xonsh, not all POSIX shell code can be used in "
"xonsh. That would defeat the purpose, and Python is better anyway! Still, "
"xonsh is compatible with shell commands in the ways that matter, such as for "
"running commands, reading the environment, and utilizing tab completion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:21
msgid ""
"The purpose of this tutorial is to teach you xonsh. There are many excellent "
"guides out there for learning Python, and this will not join their ranks. "
"Similarly, you'd probably get the most out of this tutorial if you have "
"already used a command prompt or interactive interpreter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:26
msgid "Let's dive in!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:29
msgid "Starting xonsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:30
msgid ""
"Assuming you have successfully installed xonsh, you can start up the xonsh "
"interpreter via the ``xonsh`` command. Suppose you are in a lesser terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:34
msgid ""
"$ xonsh\n"
"snail@home ~ @ █"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:39
msgid ""
"Now we are in a xonsh shell. Our username happens to be ``snail``, our "
"hostname happens to be ``home``, and we are in our home directory (``~``). "
"Alternatively, you can setup your terminal emulator (xterm, gnome-terminal, "
"etc) to run xonsh automatically when it starts up. This is recommended."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:45
msgid "Basics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:46
msgid ""
"The xonsh language is based on Python, and the xonsh shell uses Python to "
"interpret any input it receives. This makes simple things simple and we are "
"able to install and import modules, operate with values and objects, and use "
"other built-in Python functionality:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:51
msgid ""
"@ 1 + 1\n"
"\n"
"@ xpip install requests\n"
"@ import requests\n"
"  requests.get(\"https://xon.sh\").status_code\n"
"\n"
"@ print(1 if True else 2)\n"
"\n"
"@ for i, x in enumerate('xonsh'):\n"
"      # For easier indentation, Shift+Tab will enter 4 spaces.\n"
"      print(i, x)\n"
"\n"
"@ def f():\n"
"      return \"xonsh\"\n"
"  f()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:69
msgid "On the other hand, you can execute commands:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:71
msgid ""
"@ echo hello\n"
"@ cd $HOME\n"
"@ id $(whoami) > ~/id.txt\n"
"@ cat /etc/passwd | grep root"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:78
msgid "Finally, you can use everything together:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:80
msgid ""
"@ name = 'snail'\n"
"  echo @(name) > /tmp/@(name)\n"
"\n"
"@ $PATH.append('/tmp')\n"
"\n"
"@ @.imp.json.loads($(echo '{\"a\":1}'))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:89
msgid "But let's go through everything step by step."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:92
msgid "Xonsh Session Interface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:94
msgid ""
"Each session has a special global object ``@`` (we call this symbol the "
"conch) that provides instant functionality. It gives you access to different "
"parts of the current session. For example, you can use ``@.env`` to change "
"environment variables, or ``@.imp`` to import libraries. You will learn more "
"about this in the following sections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:99
msgid ""
"@ help(@)\n"
"Help on XonshSessionInterface in module xonsh.built_ins object: ...\n"
"@ @.imp.json.loads('{\"conch\":\"snail\"}')\n"
"{\"conch\":\"snail\"}\n"
"@ @.env.get('HOME')\n"
"'/home/snail'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:109
msgid "Environment Variables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:110
msgid ""
"Environment variables are written as ``$`` followed by a name.  For example, "
"``$HOME``, ``$PWD``, and ``$PATH``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:113
msgid ""
"@ $HOME\n"
"'/home/snail'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:118
msgid ""
"You can set (and export) environment variables like you would set any other "
"variable in Python.  The same is true for deleting them too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:121
msgid ""
"@ $GOAL = 'Master the shell'\n"
"@ print($GOAL)\n"
"Master the shell\n"
"@ del $GOAL\n"
"\n"
"@ $NUM = \"123\"\n"
"@ $EXT = $NUM + \"456\"\n"
"@ $EXT\n"
"'123456'\n"
"@ $FNUM = f\"{$NUM}456\"\n"
"@ $FNUM = \"{FILLME}456\".format(FILLME=$NUM)\n"
"@ $FNUM\n"
"'123456'\n"
"@ \"%s456\" % $NUM\n"
"'123456'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:139
msgid "Very nice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:143
msgid ""
"To update ``os.environ`` when the xonsh environment changes "
"set :ref:`$UPDATE_OS_ENVIRON <update_os_environ>` to ``True``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:148
msgid "The Environment Itself ``@.env``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:150
msgid ""
"All environment variables live in the built-in ``@.env`` mapping. You can "
"access this mapping directly, but in most situations, you shouldn’t need to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:153
msgid ""
"If you want for example to check if an environment variable is present in "
"your current session (say, in your awesome new ``xonsh`` script) you can use "
"the membership operator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:156
msgid ""
"@ 'HOME' in @.env\n"
"# True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:161
msgid ""
"To get information about a specific environment variable you can use "
"the :func:`~xonsh.environ.Env.help` method or just ``?`` at the end."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:164
msgid ""
"@ $AUTO_CD?\n"
"Name: $AUTO_CD\n"
"Description: Flag to enable changing to a directory by entering the dirname "
"or full path only (without the cd command).\n"
"Default: False\n"
"@ @.env.help('AUTO_CD')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:172
msgid ""
"One helpful method is :func:`~xonsh.environ.Env.swap`. It can be used to "
"temporarily set an environment variable:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:175
msgid ""
"@ with @.env.swap(SOMEVAR='foo'):\n"
"      echo $SOMEVAR\n"
"\n"
"foo\n"
"@ echo $SOMEVAR\n"
"$SOMEVAR\n"
"@"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:185
msgid "You can also change the value using preset on the command line:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:187
msgid ""
"@ $HELLO='snail' xonsh -c 'echo Hello $HELLO'\n"
"Hello snail"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:193
msgid "Environment Lookup with ``${<expr>}``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:195
msgid ""
"The ``$NAME`` is great as long as you know the name of the environment "
"variable you want to look up.  But what if you want to construct the name "
"programmatically, or read it from another variable?  Enter the ``${}`` "
"operator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:200
msgid ""
"We can place any valid Python expression inside of the curly braces in ``$"
"{<expr>}``. This result of this expression will then be used to look up a "
"value in the environment. Here are a couple of examples in action:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:204
msgid ""
"@ x = 'USER'\n"
"@ ${x}\n"
"'snail'\n"
"@ ${'HO' + 'ME'}\n"
"'/home/snail'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:212
msgid "Not bad, xonsh, not bad."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:215
msgid "Environment Types"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:217
msgid ""
"Environment variables in xonsh are not limited to strings -- they can hold "
"any Python type: strings, numbers, lists, and arbitrary objects.  When a "
"variable is used as a subprocess argument, xonsh converts it to a string "
"automatically:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:222
msgid ""
"@ $MY_STR = 'hello'\n"
"@ $MY_NUM = 42\n"
"@ $MY_LIST = [1, 2, 3]\n"
"@ showcmd echo $MY_STR $MY_NUM $MY_LIST\n"
"['echo', 'hello', '42', '[1, 2, 3]']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:230
msgid ""
"``$PATH`` is an :class:`~xonsh.environ.EnvPath` object -- a special list "
"that makes it easy to add and remove directories:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:233
msgid ""
"@ $PATH\n"
"['/usr/local/bin', '/usr/bin', '/bin']\n"
"@ $PATH.append('/opt/mytools/bin')\n"
"@ $PATH.insert(0, '$HOME/.local/bin')\n"
"@ $PATH\n"
"['/home/snail/.local/bin', '/usr/local/bin', '/usr/bin', '/bin',\n"
"'/opt/mytools/bin']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:243
msgid ""
"Any variable whose name ends in ``PATH`` or ``DIRS`` is automatically "
"treated as an ``EnvPath``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:246
msgid ""
"In subprocess mode, referencing an undefined environment variable will "
"produce an empty string.  In Python mode, however, a ``KeyError`` will be "
"raised if the variable does not exist in the environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:251
msgid ""
"You can also register custom variables with types and documentation, create "
"callable variables with dynamic values, and more -- see :doc:`env` for the "
"full details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:256
msgid "Python-mode vs Subprocess-mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:257
msgid ""
"It is sometimes helpful to make the distinction between lines that operate "
"in pure Python mode and lines that use shell-specific syntax, edit the "
"execution environment, and run commands. Unfortunately, it is not always "
"clear from the syntax alone what mode is desired. This ambiguity stems from "
"most command line utilities looking a lot like Python operators."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:263
msgid ""
"Take the case of ``ls -l``.  This is valid Python code, though it could have "
"also been written as ``ls - l`` or ``ls-l``.  So how does xonsh know that "
"``ls -l`` is meant to be run in subprocess-mode?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:267
msgid ""
"For any given line that only contains an expression statement (expr-stmt, "
"see the Python AST docs for more information), if all the names cannot be "
"found as current variables xonsh will try to parse the line as a subprocess "
"command instead.  In the above, if ``ls`` and ``l`` are not variables, then "
"subprocess mode will be attempted. If parsing in subprocess mode fails, then "
"the line is left in Python-mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:274
msgid ""
"In the following example, we will list the contents of the directory with "
"``ls -l``. Then we'll make new variable names ``ls`` and ``l`` and then "
"subtract them. Finally, we will delete ``ls`` and ``l`` and be able to list "
"the directories again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:279
msgid ""
"@ ls -l  # subproc-mode, because ls doesn't exist\n"
"-rw-rw-r-- 1 snail snail 0 Mar  8 15:46 xonsh\n"
"\n"
"@ ls = 44  # set ls and l variables to force python-mode\n"
"@ l = 2\n"
"@ ls -l\n"
"42\n"
"@ $[ls -l]  # you can still use explicit mode in scripts\n"
"-rw-rw-r-- 1 snail snail 0 Mar  8 15:46 xonsh\n"
"\n"
"@ del ls  # deleting ls will return us to subproc-mode\n"
"@ ls -l\n"
"-rw-rw-r-- 1 snail snail 0 Mar  8 15:46 xonsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:295
msgid ""
"The determination between Python- and subprocess-modes is always done in the "
"safest possible way. If anything goes wrong, it will favor Python-mode. The "
"determination between the two modes is done well ahead of any execution. You "
"do not need to worry about partially executed commands - that is impossible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:301
msgid ""
"If you would like to explicitly run a subprocess command, you can always use "
"the formal xonsh subprocess syntax that we will see in the following "
"sections. For example: ``$[ls -l]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:307
msgid "Subprocess"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:310
msgid "Running Commands"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:311
msgid ""
"As a shell, xonsh is meant to make running commands easy and fun. Running "
"subprocess commands should work like in any other shell."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:314
msgid ""
"@ echo \"Yoo hoo\"\n"
"Yoo hoo\n"
"@ cd xonsh\n"
"@ ls\n"
"build  docs     README.rst  setup.py  xonsh           __pycache__\n"
"dist   LICENSE  scripts     tests     xonsh.egg-info\n"
"@ dir scripts\n"
"xonsh  xonsh.bat\n"
"@ git status\n"
"On branch main\n"
"@ exit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:328
msgid "This should feel very natural."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:332
msgid ""
"Access the last run subprocess command using ``@.lastcmd``; e.g. to get the "
"return code, run ``@.lastcmd.rtn``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:337
msgid "Strings and Quoting in Subprocess Mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:339
msgid ""
"Single or double quotes can be used to remove the special meaning of certain "
"characters or words to xonsh. If a subprocess command contains characters "
"that collide with xonsh syntax then quotes must be used to force xonsh to "
"not interpret them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:344
msgid ""
"@ echo ${\n"
"SyntaxError\n"
"@ echo '${'\n"
"${"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:351
msgid ""
"The contents of the string are passed directly to the subprocess command as "
"a single argument.  So whenever you are in doubt, or if there is a xonsh "
"syntax error because of a filename, just wrap the offending portion in a "
"string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:355
msgid "A common use case for this is files with spaces in their names:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:357
msgid ""
"@ touch \"sp ace\"\n"
"@ ls -l\n"
"total 0\n"
"-rw-rw-r-- 1 snail snail 0 Mar  8 17:50 sp ace\n"
"-rw-rw-r-- 1 snail snail 0 Mar  8 15:46 xonsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:365
msgid ""
"By default, the name of an environment variable inside a string will be "
"replaced by the contents of that variable (in subprocess mode only).  For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:369
msgid ""
"@ print(\"my home is $HOME\")\n"
"my home is $HOME\n"
"@ echo \"my home is $HOME\"\n"
"my home is /home/snail"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:376
msgid ""
"You can avoid this expansion within a particular command by forcing the "
"strings to be evaluated in Python mode using the ``@()`` syntax:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:379
msgid ""
"@ echo \"my home is $HOME\"\n"
"my home is /home/snail\n"
"@ echo @(\"my home is $HOME\")\n"
"my home is $HOME"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:389
msgid ""
"You can also disable environment variable expansion completely by setting "
"``$EXPAND_ENV_VARS`` to ``False``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:393
msgid "Xonsh supports Python string prefixes in subprocess arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:395
msgid "``r\"\"`` — raw, no escapes (``r'\\n'`` stays as ``\\n``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:396
msgid "``f\"\"`` — formatted, with ``{expr}`` substitution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:397
msgid "``p\"\"`` — path, returns ``pathlib.Path`` with ``$ENV`` expansion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:399
msgid ""
"These can be combined (``fr\"\"``, ``pf\"\"``, ``pr\"\"``). For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:401
msgid ""
"@ echo r'no\\escape'\n"
"no\\escape\n"
"@ echo f\"{'hello':>10}\"\n"
"     hello\n"
"@ p\"/tmp\" / \"file.txt\"\n"
"PosixPath('/tmp/file.txt')\n"
"@ name = \"docs\"\n"
"@ pf\"$HOME/{name}\"\n"
"PosixPath('/Users/snail/docs')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:413
msgid ""
"See :doc:`strings` for the full reference table of how each prefix affects "
"environment variable substitution, brace formatting, and escapes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:419
msgid "Captured Subprocess with ``$()`` and ``!()``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:420
msgid ""
"The ``$(<expr>)`` operator in xonsh executes a subprocess command and "
"*captures* some information about that command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:423
msgid ""
"The ``$()`` syntax captures and returns the standard output stream of the "
"command as a Python string. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:426
msgid ""
"@ $(ls -l)\n"
"'total 0\\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 snail snail 0 Mar  8 15:46 xonsh\\n'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:434
msgid ""
"By default the output is represented as one single block of output with new "
"line characters. You can set ``$XONSH_SUBPROC_OUTPUT_FORMAT`` to "
"``list_lines`` to have a list of distinct lines in the commands like ``du -h "
"$(ls)``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:439
msgid ""
"The ``!()`` syntax captured more information about the command, as an "
"instance of a class called ``CommandPipeline``.  This object contains more "
"information about the result of the given command, including the return "
"code, the process id, the standard output and standard error streams, and "
"information about how input and output were redirected.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:445
msgid ""
"@ !(ls nonexistent_directory)\n"
"CommandPipeline(\n"
"   pid=26968,\n"
"   returncode=2,\n"
"   args=['ls', 'nonexistent_directory'],\n"
"   alias=['ls', '--color=auto', '-v'],\n"
"   timestamps=[1485235484.5016758, None],\n"
"   executed_cmd=['ls', '--color=auto', '-v', 'nonexistent_directory'],\n"
"   input=None,\n"
"   output=,\n"
"   errors=None\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:460
msgid ""
"The captured object ``!()`` operator allows for non-blocking execution. You "
"can call a long-running command, intersperse other commands and read the "
"captured output later:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:464
msgid ""
"@ p = !(echo snail)\n"
"@ p.output\n"
"''\n"
"@ p.end()\n"
"@ p.output\n"
"'snail'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:473
msgid ""
"You can force ``xonsh`` to block and wait for the command to complete by "
"asking for the return code, printing the object or reading the ``out`` "
"attribute:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:476
msgid ""
"@ p = !(echo snail)\n"
"@ p.out\n"
"'snail'\n"
"@ p = !(echo party)\n"
"@ p.rtn\n"
"0\n"
"@ p.output\n"
"'party'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:487
msgid ""
"This object will be \"truthy\" if its return code was 0, and it is equal "
"(via ``==``) to its return code. It also hashes to its return code. "
"Converting the object to the string will return the output. This allows for "
"some interesting new kinds of interactions with subprocess commands, for "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:492
msgid ""
"def check_file(file):\n"
"    if !(test -e @(file)):\n"
"        if !(test -f @(file)) or !(test -d @(file)):\n"
"            print(\"File is a regular file or directory\")\n"
"        else:\n"
"            print(\"File is not a regular file or directory\")\n"
"    else:\n"
"        print(\"File does not exist\")\n"
"\n"
"def wait_until_google_responds():\n"
"    while not !(ping -c 1 google.com):\n"
"        sleep 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:508
msgid ""
"If you iterate over the ``CommandPipeline`` object, it will yield lines of "
"its output.  Using this, you can quickly and cleanly process output from "
"commands. Additionally, these objects expose a method ``itercheck``, which "
"behaves the same as the built-in iterator but raises "
"``XonshCalledProcessError`` if the process had a nonzero return code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:514
msgid ""
"def get_wireless_interface():\n"
"    \"\"\"Returns devicename of first connected wifi, None otherwise\"\"\"\n"
"    for line in !(nmcli device):\n"
"        dev, typ, state, conn_name = line.split(None, 3)\n"
"        if typ == 'wifi' and state == 'connected':\n"
"            return dev\n"
"\n"
"def grep_path(path, regexp):\n"
"    \"\"\"Recursively greps `path` for perl `regexp`\n"
"\n"
"    Returns a dict of 'matches' and 'failures'.\n"
"    Matches are files that contain the given regexp.\n"
"    Failures are files that couldn't be scanned.\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    matches = []\n"
"    failures = []\n"
"\n"
"    try:\n"
"        for match in !(grep -RPl @(regexp) @(str(path))).itercheck():\n"
"            matches.append(match)\n"
"    except XonshCalledProcessError as error:\n"
"        for line in error.stderr.split('\\n'):\n"
"            if not line.strip():\n"
"                continue\n"
"            filename = line.split('grep: ', 1)[1].rsplit(':', 1)[0]\n"
"            failures.append(filename)\n"
"    return {'matches': matches, 'failures': failures}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:545
msgid ""
"The ``$()`` and ``!()`` operators are expressions themselves. This means "
"that we can assign the results to a variable or perform any other "
"manipulations we want."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:549
msgid ""
"@ x = $(ls -l)\n"
"@ print(x.upper())\n"
"TOTAL 0\n"
"-RW-RW-R-- 1 SNAIL SNAIL 0 MAR  8 15:46 XONSH\n"
"@ y = !(ls -l)\n"
"@ print(y.returncode)\n"
"0\n"
"@ print(y.rtn)  # alias to returncode\n"
"0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:562
msgid "Job control is not implemented for captured subprocesses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:564
msgid ""
"While in subprocess-mode or inside of a captured subprocess, we can always "
"still query the environment with ``$NAME`` variables or the ``${}`` syntax, "
"or inject Python values with the ``@()`` operator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:568
msgid ""
"@ $(echo $HOME)\n"
"'/home/snail'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:574
msgid "Threading"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:576
msgid ""
"If you want to work more closely with captured commands, you need to know "
"about threading. Xonsh has a threading prediction mechanism that allows it "
"to understand which commands can capture everything. For example, the "
"``echo`` command has no interaction with the user and is capturable. "
"However, some tools have mixed behavior and can be run for either "
"interactive or non-interactive tasks. The best example of this is ``ssh``, "
"which allows for remote terminal sessions and executing commands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:582
msgid ""
"To handle different types of tasks, xonsh has the ``@thread`` and "
"``@unthread`` built-in decorator aliases. If you need to capture the output "
"from an interactive tool that has a capturable mode use ``@thread`` to run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:585
msgid ""
"@ !(@thread ssh host -T 'echo remote')\n"
"CommandPipeline(output=\"remote\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:592
msgid "Uncaptured Subprocess with ``$[]`` and ``![]``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:593
msgid ""
"Uncaptured subprocesses are denoted with the ``$[]`` and ``![]`` operators. "
"They are the same as ``$()`` captured subprocesses in almost every way. The "
"only difference is that the subprocess's stdout passes directly through "
"xonsh and to the screen.  The return value of ``$[]`` is always ``None``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:598
msgid ""
"In the following, we can see that the results of ``$[]`` are automatically "
"printed, and that the return value is not a string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:601
msgid ""
"@ x = $[ls -l]\n"
"total 0\n"
"-rw-rw-r-- 1 snail snail 0 Mar  8 15:46 xonsh\n"
"@ x is None\n"
"True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:609
msgid ""
"The ``![]`` operator is similar to the ``!()`` in that it returns an object "
"containing information about the result of executing the given command. "
"However, its standard output and standard error streams are directed to the "
"terminal, and the resulting object is not displayed.  For example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:614
msgid ""
"@ x = ![ls -l] and ![echo \"hi\"]\n"
"total 0\n"
"-rw-rw-r-- 1 snail snail 0 Mar  8 15:46 xonsh\n"
"hi"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:623
msgid "Python Evaluation with ``@()``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:625
msgid ""
"The ``@(<expr>)`` operator form works in subprocess mode, and will evaluate "
"arbitrary Python code. The result is appended to the subprocess command "
"list. If the result is a string or bytes, it is appended to the argument "
"list. If the result is a list or other non-string sequence, the contents are "
"converted to strings and appended to the argument list in order. If the "
"result in the first position is a function, it is treated as an alias (see "
"the section on `Aliases`_ below), even if it was not explicitly added to the "
"``aliases`` mapping.  Otherwise, the result is automatically converted to a "
"string. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:634
msgid ""
"@ x = 'xonsh'\n"
"@ y = 'party'\n"
"@ echo @(x + ' ' + y)\n"
"xonsh party\n"
"@ echo @(2+2)\n"
"4\n"
"@ echo @([42, 'yo'])  # `echo` with two arguments.\n"
"42 yo\n"
"@ echo \"hello\" | @(lambda args, stdin=None: stdin.read().strip() + \" "
"world\\n\")\n"
"hello world\n"
"@ @(['echo', 'hello', 'world'])  # List of arguments.\n"
"hello world\n"
"@ @('echo hello world')  # Single argument.\n"
"xonsh: subprocess mode: command not found: echo hello world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:651
msgid ""
"This syntax can be used inside of a captured or uncaptured subprocess, and "
"can be used to generate any of the tokens in the subprocess command list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:654
msgid ""
"@ out = $(echo @(x + ' ' + y))\n"
"@ out\n"
"'xonsh party'\n"
"@ @(\"ech\" + \"o\") \"hey\"\n"
"hey"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:662
msgid ""
"Thus, ``@()`` allows us to create complex commands in Python-mode and then "
"feed them to a subprocess as needed.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:665
msgid ""
"for i in range(20):\n"
"    $[touch @('file%02d' % i)]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:670
msgid ""
"The ``@()`` syntax may also be used inside of subprocess arguments, not just "
"as a stand-alone argument. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:673
msgid ""
"@ x = 'hello'\n"
"@ echo /path/to/@(x)\n"
"/path/to/hello"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:679
msgid ""
"When used inside of a subprocess argument and ``<expr>`` evaluates to a non-"
"string iterable, ``@()`` will expand to the outer product of all given "
"values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:683
msgid ""
"@ echo /path/to/@(['hello', 'world'])\n"
"/path/to/hello /path/to/world\n"
"\n"
"@ echo @(['a', 'b']):@('x', 'y')\n"
"a:x a:y b:x b:y"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:693
msgid "Command Substitution with ``@$()``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:695
msgid ""
"A common use of the ``@()`` and ``$()`` operators is allowing the output of "
"a command to replace the command itself (command substitution): "
"``@([i.strip() for i in $(cmd).split()])``.  Xonsh offers a short-hand "
"syntax for this operation: ``@$(cmd)``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:700
msgid "Consider the following example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:702
msgid ""
"@ # this returns a string representing stdout\n"
"@ $(which ls)\n"
"'ls --color=auto'\n"
"\n"
"@ # this attempts to run the command, but as one argument\n"
"@ # (looks for 'ls --color=auto' with spaces)\n"
"@ @($(which ls))\n"
"xonsh: subprocess mode: command not found: ls --color=auto\n"
"\n"
"@ # this actually executes the intended command\n"
"@ @([i.strip() for i in $(which ls).split()])\n"
"some_file  some_other_file\n"
"\n"
"@ # this does the same thing, but is much more concise\n"
"@ @$(which ls)\n"
"some_file  some_other_file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:723
msgid "Nesting Subprocesses"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:724
msgid ""
"Though I am begging you not to abuse this, it is possible to nest the "
"subprocess operators that we have seen so far (``$()``, ``$[]``, ``${}``, "
"``@()``, ``@$()``).  An instance of ``ls -l`` that is on the wrong side of "
"the border of the absurd is shown below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:729
msgid ""
"@ $[@$(which @($(echo ls).strip())) @('-' + $(printf 'l'))]\n"
"total 0\n"
"-rw-rw-r-- 1 snail snail 0 Mar  8 15:46 xonsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:735
msgid "With great power, and so forth..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:737
msgid ""
"Nesting these subprocess operators inside of ``$()`` and/or ``$[]`` works "
"because the contents of those operators are executed in subprocess mode.  "
"Since ``@()`` and ``${}`` run their contents in Python mode, it is not "
"possible to nest other subprocess operators inside of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:743
msgid ""
"To understand how xonsh executes the subprocess commands try to "
"set :ref:`$XONSH_SUBPROC_TRACE <xonsh_subproc_trace>` to ``True``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:746
msgid ""
"@ $XONSH_SUBPROC_TRACE = True\n"
"@ $[@$(which @($(echo ls).strip())) @('-' + $(printf 'l'))]\n"
"TRACE SUBPROC: (['echo', 'ls'],)\n"
"TRACE SUBPROC: (['which', 'ls'],)\n"
"TRACE SUBPROC: (['printf', 'l'],)\n"
"TRACE SUBPROC: (['ls', '--color=auto', '-v', '-l'],)\n"
"total 0\n"
"-rw-rw-r-- 1 snail snail 0 Mar  8 15:46 xonsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:759
msgid "Pipes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:761
msgid ""
"In subprocess-mode, xonsh allows you to use the ``|`` character to pipe "
"together commands as you would in other shells."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:764
msgid ""
"@ env | uniq | sort | grep PATH\n"
"DATAPATH=/usr/share/MCNPX/v260/Data/\n"
"DEFAULTS_PATH=/usr/share/gconf/awesome-gnome.default.path\n"
"LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/home/snail/.local/lib:\n"
"MANDATORY_PATH=/usr/share/gconf/awesome-gnome.mandatory.path\n"
"PATH=/home/snail/.local/bin:/home/snail/sandbox/bin:/usr/local/bin\n"
"XDG_SEAT_PATH=/org/freedesktop/DisplayManager/Seat0\n"
"XDG_SESSION_PATH=/org/freedesktop/DisplayManager/Session0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:775
msgid ""
"This is only available in subprocess-mode because ``|`` is otherwise a "
"Python operator. If you are unsure of what pipes are, there are many great "
"references out there. You should be able to find information on "
"StackOverflow or Google."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:781
msgid "Logical Subprocess And/Or"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:783
msgid ""
"Subprocess-mode also allows you to use the ``and`` operator to chain "
"together subprocess commands. The truth value of a command is evaluated as "
"whether its return code is zero (i.e. ``proc.returncode == 0``).  Like in "
"Python, if the command evaluates to ``False``, subsequent commands will not "
"be executed. For example, suppose we want to lists files that may or may not "
"exist:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:789
msgid ""
"@ touch exists\n"
"@ ls exists and ls doesnt\n"
"exists\n"
"/bin/ls: cannot access doesnt: No such file or directory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:796
msgid ""
"However, if you list the file that doesn't exist first, you would have only "
"seen the error:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:799
msgid ""
"@ ls doesnt and ls exists\n"
"/bin/ls: cannot access doesnt: No such file or directory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:804
msgid ""
"Also, don't worry. Xonsh directly translates the ``&&`` operator into "
"``and`` for you. It is less Pythonic, of course, but it is your shell!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:807
msgid ""
"Much like with ``and``, you can use the ``or`` operator to chain together "
"subprocess commands. The difference, to be certain, is that subsequent "
"commands will be executed only if the if the return code is non-zero (i.e. a "
"failure). Using the file example from above:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:813
msgid ""
"@ ls exists or ls doesnt\n"
"exists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:818
msgid ""
"This doesn't even try to list a non-existent file! However, if you list the "
"file that doesn't exist first, you will see the error and then the file that "
"does exist:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:822
msgid ""
"@ ls doesnt or ls exists\n"
"#/bin/ls: cannot access doesnt: No such file or directory\n"
"exists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:828
msgid "Xonsh also directly translates the ``||`` operator into ``or``, too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:831
msgid "Input/Output Redirection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:833
msgid ""
"xonsh also allows you to redirect ``stdin``, ``stdout``, and/or ``stderr``. "
"This allows you to control where the output of a command is sent, and where "
"it receives its input from.  xonsh has its own syntax for these operations, "
"but, for compatibility purposes, xonsh also support POSIX-like syntax."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:838
msgid ""
"The basic operations are \"write to\" (``>``), \"append to\" (``>>``), and "
"\"read from\" (``<``).  The details of these are perhaps best explained "
"through examples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:842
msgid ""
"The target of the redirection should be separated by a space, otherwise "
"xonsh will raise a SyntaxError."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:845
msgid ""
"Redirecting ``stdout``. The operators ``>``, ``out>``, ``o>``, and ``1>`` "
"(POSIX) all execute ``cmd`` and write its regular output (stdout) to a file, "
"creating it if it does not exist:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:849
msgid "@ cmd > output.txt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:853
msgid ""
"These can be made to append to ``output.txt`` instead of overwriting its "
"contents by replacing ``>`` with ``>>`` (note that ``>>`` will still create "
"the file if it does not exist)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:857
msgid ""
"Redirecting ``stderr``. The operators ``err>``, ``e>``, and ``2>`` (POSIX) "
"all execute ``cmd`` and write its error output (stderr) to a file, creating "
"it if it does not exist:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:861
msgid "@ cmd err> errors.txt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:865
msgid ""
"As above, replacing ``>`` with ``>>`` will cause the error output to be "
"appended to ``errors.txt``, rather than replacing its contents."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:868
msgid ""
"Combining streams. The operators ``all>``, ``a>``, and ``&>`` (POSIX) all "
"send both regular output and error output to the same location:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:871
msgid "@ cmd all> combined.txt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:875
msgid ""
"Merging stderr into stdout. The operators ``err>out``, ``err>o``, ``e>out``, "
"``e>o``, and ``2>&1`` (POSIX) all explicitly merge stderr into stdout so "
"that error messages are reported to the same location as regular output:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:879
msgid ""
"@ cmd err>out\n"
"@ cmd err>out | cmd2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:885
msgid ""
"Merging stdout into stderr. Similarly, the operators ``out>err``, ``out>e``, "
"``o>err``, ``o>e``, and ``1>&2`` (POSIX) all send stdout to stderr:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:888
msgid "@ cmd out>err"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:892
msgid ""
"Routing streams into a pipe. The operators ``a>p`` (``all>p``) and ``e>p`` "
"(``err>p``) add stderr to the following ``|`` pipe. The pipe still carries "
"stdout as usual, unless an explicit ``o> file`` diverts stdout elsewhere — "
"which makes ``e>p`` useful for the pattern of sending stdout to a file while "
"stderr flows into the pipe. These operators require a following pipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:898
msgid ""
"@ cmd a>p | cmd2                     # stdout + stderr into the pipe\n"
"@ cmd e>p | grep warning             # same — pipe carries both streams\n"
"@ cmd o> out.txt e>p | grep warning  # stdout to file, stderr into the pipe"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:904
msgid ""
"Redirecting ``stdin`` is also possible to have a command read its input from "
"a file, rather than from ``stdin``.  The following examples demonstrate two "
"ways to accomplish this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:907
msgid ""
"@ cmd < input.txt\n"
"@ < input.txt cmd"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:912
msgid ""
"Combining I/O redirects is also possible.  Below is one example of a "
"complicated redirect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:914
msgid "@ cmd1 e>o < input.txt | cmd2 > output.txt e>> errors.txt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:918
msgid ""
"This line will run ``cmd1`` with the contents of ``input.txt`` fed in on "
"stdin, and will pipe all output (stdout and stderr) to ``cmd2``; the regular "
"output of this command will be redirected to ``output.txt``, and the error "
"output will be appended to ``errors.txt``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:925
msgid "Job Control"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:927
msgid ""
"You can get a listing of all currently running jobs with the ``jobs`` "
"command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:929
msgid ""
"Each job has a unique identifier (starting with 1 and counting upward).  By "
"default, the ``fg`` and ``bg`` commands operate on the job that was started "
"most recently.  You can bring older jobs to the foreground or background by "
"specifying the appropriate ID; for example, ``fg 1`` brings the job with ID "
"1 to the foreground. Additionally, specify \"+\" for the most recent job and "
"\"-\" for the second most recent job."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:937
msgid "Background Jobs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:939
msgid ""
"Typically, when you start a program running in xonsh, xonsh itself will "
"pause and wait for that program to terminate.  Sometimes, though, you may "
"want to continue giving commands to xonsh while that program is running.  In "
"subprocess mode, you can start a process \"in the background\" (i.e., in a "
"way that allows continued use of the shell) by adding an ampersand (``&``) "
"to the end of your command.  Background jobs are very useful when running "
"programs with graphical user interfaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:947
msgid "The following shows an example with ``emacs``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:949
msgid ""
"@ emacs &\n"
"@"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:954
msgid "Note that the prompt is returned to you after emacs is started."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:956
msgid ""
"Normally background commands end upon the shell closing. To allow a "
"background command to continue running after the shell has exited, use the "
"``disown`` command which accepts either no arguments (to disown the most "
"recent job) or an arbitrary number of job identifiers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:962
msgid "Foreground Jobs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:964
msgid ""
"If you start a program in the foreground (with no ampersand), you can "
"suspend that program's execution and return to the xonsh prompt by pressing "
"Control-Z. This will give control of the terminal back to xonsh, and will "
"keep the program paused in the background."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:969
msgid ""
"Suspending processes via Control-Z is not yet supported when running on "
"Windows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:972
msgid ""
"To unpause the program and bring it back to the foreground, you can use the "
"``fg`` command.  To unpause the program have it continue in the background "
"(giving you continued access to the xonsh prompt), you can use the ``bg`` "
"command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:979
msgid "Filename Globbing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:982
msgid "Normal Globbing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:983
msgid ""
"Filename globbing with the ``*`` character is also allowed in subprocess-"
"mode. This simply uses Python's glob module under-the-covers.  See there for "
"more details.  As an example, start with a lovely bunch of xonshs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:987
msgid ""
"@ touch xonsh conch konk quanxh\n"
"@ ls\n"
"conch  konk  quanxh  xonsh\n"
"@ ls *h\n"
"conch  quanxh  xonsh\n"
"@ ls *o*\n"
"conch  konk  xonsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:997
msgid ""
"In subprocess mode, normal globbing happens without any special syntax. "
"However, there is backtick syntax that is available inside Python mode as "
"well as subprocess mode. This can be done using ``g````:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1001
msgid ""
"@ touch a aa aaa aba abba aab aabb abcba\n"
"@ ls a*b*\n"
"aab  aabb  aba  abba  abcba\n"
"@ ls g`a*b*`\n"
"aab  aabb  aba  abba  abcba\n"
"@ print(g`a*b*`)\n"
"['aab', 'aabb', 'abba', 'abcba', 'aba']\n"
"@ len(g`a*b*`)\n"
"5"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1014
msgid "Regular Expression Globbing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1016
msgid ""
"If you have ever felt that normal globbing could use some more octane, then "
"regex globbing is the tool for you! Any string that uses backticks (`````) "
"instead of quotes (``'``, ``\"``) is interpreted as a regular expression to "
"match filenames against.  Like with regular globbing, a list of successful "
"matches is returned.  In Python-mode, this is just a list of strings. In "
"subprocess-mode, each filename becomes its own argument to the subprocess "
"command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1024
msgid "Let's see a demonstration with some simple filenames:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1027
msgid ""
"@ touch a aa aaa aba abba aab aabb abcba\n"
"@ ls `a(a+|b+)a`\n"
"aaa  aba  abba\n"
"@ print(`a(a+|b+)a`)\n"
"['aaa', 'aba', 'abba']\n"
"@ len(`a(a+|b+)a`)\n"
"3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1037
msgid ""
"This same kind of search is performed if the backticks are prefaced with "
"``r``. So the following expressions are equivalent: ```test``` and "
"``r`test```."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1040
msgid ""
"Other than the regex matching, this functions in the same way as normal "
"globbing.  For more information, please see the documentation for the ``re`` "
"module in the Python standard library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1046
msgid "Formatted Glob Literals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1048
msgid ""
"Using the ``f`` modifier with either regex or normal globbing makes the glob "
"pattern behave like a formatted string literal. This can be used to "
"substitute variables and other expressions into the glob pattern:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1052
msgid ""
"@ touch a aa aaa aba abba aab aabb abcba\n"
"@ mypattern = 'ab'\n"
"@ print(f`{mypattern[0]}+`)\n"
"['a', 'aa', 'aaa']\n"
"@ print(gf`{mypattern}*`)\n"
"['aba', 'abba', 'abcba']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1063
msgid "Match Globbing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1065
msgid ""
"The ``m`` modifier enables match globbing — a regex glob that returns "
"capture groups instead of full paths. This is useful for extracting parts of "
"matched paths directly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1069
msgid ""
"@ for parent, name in m`tests/(.*)/(test_.*\\.py)`:\n"
"      print(parent, name)\n"
"completers test_python.py\n"
"completers test_path_completers.py\n"
"procs test_specs.py\n"
"procs test_pipes.py"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1078
msgid "With a single capture group, a flat list of strings is returned:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1080
msgid ""
"@ m`xonsh/(.*\\.py)`.sorted().files()\n"
"['__init__.py', '__main__.py', 'aliases.py']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1085
msgid ""
"See :doc:`globbing` for the full ``m`` glob reference and ``XonshList`` "
"methods."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1089
msgid "Custom Path Searches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1091
msgid ""
"In addition, if normal globbing and regular expression globbing are not "
"enough, xonsh allows you to specify your own search functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1094
msgid ""
"A search function is defined as a function of a single argument (a string) "
"that returns a list of possible matches to that string.  Search functions "
"can then be used with backticks with the following syntax: ``@<name>`test```"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1098
msgid "The following example shows the form of these functions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1100
msgid ""
"@ def foo(s):\n"
"      return [i for i in os.listdir('.') if i.startswith(s)]\n"
"@ @foo`aa`\n"
"['aa', 'aaa', 'aab', 'aabb']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1109
msgid "Path Output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1111
msgid ""
"Using the ``p`` modifier with either regex or glob backticks changes the "
"return type from a list of strings to a list of :class:`pathlib.Path` "
"objects:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1114
msgid ""
"@ p`.*`\n"
"[Path('foo'), Path('bar')]\n"
"@ [x for x in pg`**` if x.is_symlink()]\n"
"[Path('a_link')]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1123
msgid "Path Literals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1125
msgid ""
"Path objects can be instantiated directly using *p-string* syntax. Path "
"objects can be converted back to plain strings with `str()`, and this "
"conversion is handled implicitly in subprocess mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1129
msgid ""
"@ mypath = p'/foo/bar'\n"
"@ mypath\n"
"Path('/foo/bar')\n"
"@ mypath.stem\n"
"'bar'\n"
"@ echo @(mypath)\n"
"/foo/bar\n"
"@ pwd\n"
"/home/snail\n"
"\n"
"@ with p'/tmp'.cd():\n"
"      pwd\n"
"/tmp\n"
"\n"
"@ with p'/tmp/newdir'.mkdir(mode=0o777, parents=True, exist_ok=True).cd():\n"
"      pwd\n"
"/tmp/newdir\n"
"\n"
"@ p'/tmp/new.txt'.touch().chmod(0o700).write_text('hello')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1151
msgid ""
"Path object allows do some tricks with paths. Globbing certain path, "
"checking and getting info:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1153
msgid ""
"@ mypath = p'/etc'\n"
"@ sorted(mypath.glob('**/*xonshrc*'))\n"
"[Path('/etc/xonsh/xonshrc'), Path('/etc/xonsh/rc.d/xonshrc.xsh')]\n"
"@ [mypath.exists(), mypath.is_dir(), mypath.is_file(), mypath.parent, "
"mypath.owner()]\n"
"[True, True, False, Path('/'), 'root']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1163
msgid "Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1164
msgid ""
"Another important xonsh built-in is the ``aliases`` mapping.  This is like a "
"dictionary that affects how subprocess commands are run.  If you are "
"familiar with the POSIX shells ``alias`` built-in, this is similar.  Alias "
"command matching only occurs for the first element of a subprocess command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1170
msgid "Register an Alias"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1172
msgid ""
"The keys of ``aliases`` are strings that act as commands in subprocess-mode. "
"The values are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1175
msgid ""
"A list of strings where the first element is the command and the remaining "
"elements are its arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1176
msgid ""
"A simple string that is automatically converted into a list using xonsh’s "
"``Lexer.split()`` method."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1177
msgid ""
"A string representing a xonsh command that will be converted into an "
"``ExecAlias`` (details next)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1178
msgid "A callable that will be used as a callable alias (details next)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1180
msgid ""
"@ aliases['ls']\n"
"['ls', '--color=auto', '-v']\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases['e'] = 'echo echo'\n"
"@ aliases['ll'] = ['ls', '-la']\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases |= {\n"
"    'g':   'git status -sb',\n"
"    'gp':  ['git', 'pull'],\n"
"    'gco': 'git checkout',\n"
"  }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1194
msgid ""
"If you were to run ``gco feature-fabulous`` with the above aliases in "
"effect, the command would reduce to ``['git', 'checkout', 'feature-"
"fabulous']`` before being executed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1198
msgid ""
"Removing an alias is as easy as deleting the key from the alias dictionary:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1200
msgid "@ del aliases['banana']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1205
msgid "Alias to Modify Command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1207
msgid ""
"The best way to modify command on the fly is to use alias that returns "
"modified command. One of the most interesting application is expanding an "
"alias:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1210
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  @aliases.return_command\n"
"  def _xsudo(args):\n"
"      \"\"\"Sudo with expanding aliases.\"\"\"\n"
"      if args:\n"
"          return ['sudo', '--', *aliases.eval_alias(args)]\n"
"      else:\n"
"          return ['sudo', '--']  # Allows completion to work properly.\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases['install'] = \"apt install cowsay\"\n"
"@ xsudo install\n"
"Password:\n"
"Install cowsay"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1226
msgid "Or implement logic to run the right command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1228
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  @aliases.return_command\n"
"  def _vi(args):\n"
"      \"\"\"Universal vi editor.\"\"\"\n"
"      if $(which vim 2>/dev/null):\n"
"          return ['vim'] + args\n"
"      else:\n"
"          return ['vi'] + args\n"
"\n"
"@ vi file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1242
msgid ""
"See `Return Command Aliases <https://xon.sh/dev/callable_aliases.html#return-"
"command-aliases>`_ for the full reference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1247
msgid "Callable Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1250
msgid "Basic Callable Alias"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1252
msgid ""
"A callable alias is a function (or callable object) with a specific "
"signature that can be used as a subprocess, either directly or when "
"registered as an alias."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1254
msgid "Using directly with Python evaluation via ``@()``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1256
msgid ""
"@ def mybox():\n"
"     print('apple')\n"
"     echo 'banana'\n"
"\n"
"@ @(mybox) | grep ba\n"
"banana"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1265
msgid "Register callable as an alias:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1267
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register('mybox')\n"
"  def _mybox():\n"
"     print('apple')\n"
"     echo 'banana'\n"
"\n"
"@ mybox | grep ba\n"
"banana\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases['hello'] = lambda: print(f'Hello world')\n"
"  hello\n"
"Hello world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1282
msgid "ExecAlias"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1284
msgid ""
"If the string is representing a block of xonsh code, the alias will be "
"registered as an ``ExecAlias``, which is a callable alias under the hood. "
"This block of code will then be executed whenever the alias is run. The "
"arguments are available in the list ``$args`` or by the index in ``$arg<n>`` "
"environment variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1289
msgid ""
"@ aliases |= {\n"
"    'answer': 'echo @(21+21)',\n"
"    'piu':    'pip install -U @($args)',\n"
"    'cdls':   'cd $arg0 && ls',\n"
"  }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1297
msgid ""
"You need to add ``@($args)`` manually if you need arguments in ExecAlias:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1299
msgid ""
"@ aliases |= {\n"
"    'noargs': 'echo @(\"all args will be ignored\")',\n"
"    'args':  'echo @(\"the arguments are:\") @($args)',\n"
"  }\n"
"@ noargs 1 2 3\n"
"all args will be ignored\n"
"@ args 1 2 3\n"
"the arguments are: 1 2 3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1310
msgid "These three definitions are equal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1312
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _answer():\n"
"      echo @(21+21)\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases['answer'] = lambda: $[echo @(21+21)]\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases['answer'] = 'echo @(21+21)'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1324
msgid "Anonymous Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1325
msgid ""
"As mentioned above, it is also possible to treat functions outside this "
"mapping as aliases, by wrapping them in ``@()``.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1328
msgid ""
"@ @(_banana)\n"
"'My spoon is tooo big!'\n"
"@ echo \"hello\" | @(lambda args, stdin=None: stdin.read().strip() + ' ' + "
"args[0] + '\\n') world\n"
"hello world"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1337
msgid "Unthreadable Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1338
msgid ""
"Usually, callable alias commands will be run in a separate thread so that "
"they may be run in the background.  However, some aliases may need to be "
"executed on the thread that they were called from. This is mostly useful for "
"interactive tools (vim, less, htop), debuggers and profilers. To make an "
"alias run in the foreground, use the ``@aliases.unthreadable`` decorator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1345
msgid ""
"@aliases.register\n"
"@aliases.unthreadable\n"
"def _mycmd(args, stdin=None):\n"
"    return 'In your face!'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1354
msgid "Uncapturable Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1355
msgid ""
"Also, callable aliases by default will be executed such that their output is "
"captured (like most commands in xonsh that don't enter alternate mode). "
"However, some aliases may want to run alternate-mode commands themselves. "
"Thus the callable alias can't be captured without dire consequences (tm). To "
"prevent this, you can declare a callable alias uncapturable. This is mostly "
"useful for aliases that then open up text editors, pagers, or the like. To "
"make an alias uncapturable, use the ``@aliases.uncapturable`` decorator. "
"This is probably best used in conjunction with ``@aliases.unthreadable``. "
"For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1365
msgid ""
"@aliases.register\n"
"@aliases.uncapturable\n"
"@aliases.unthreadable\n"
"def _binvi(args, stdin=None):\n"
"    vi -b @(args)  # Edit binary files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1373
msgid ""
"Note that ``@()`` is required to pass the python list ``args`` to a "
"subprocess command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1377
msgid "Click Integration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1378
msgid ""
"If the `click <https://click.palletsprojects.com/>`_ package is installed, "
"you can register a click command as a xonsh alias with "
"``@aliases.register_click_command``. The ``aliases.click`` attribute exposes "
"the ``click`` module itself, so ``@aliases.click.option(...)`` works without "
"a separate ``import click``. Both are loaded lazily on first access."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1384
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register_click_command\n"
"  @aliases.click.option('--name', help='The person to greet.')\n"
"  @aliases.click.option('--count', default=1, help='Number of greetings.')\n"
"  def _hello(ctx, count, name):\n"
"      \"\"\"Greets NAME for a total of COUNT times.\"\"\"\n"
"      for i in range(count):\n"
"          print(name, file=ctx.stdout)\n"
"\n"
"@ hello --count 3 --name World\n"
"World\n"
"World\n"
"World"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1399
msgid ""
"The call forms mirror ``@aliases.register`` — bare, ``()``, or with an "
"explicit name. Inside the click callback, ``ctx`` is a ``click.Context`` "
"subclass carrying the usual xonsh alias parameters as attributes "
"(``ctx.alias_args``, ``ctx.stdin``, ``ctx.stdout``, ``ctx.env``, and so on). "
"See :doc:`callable_aliases` for the full reference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1406
msgid "Command Decorators (Decorator Aliases)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1407
msgid ""
"In xonsh you can decorate the command to transform output into desired "
"object:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1409
msgid ""
"@ $(@lines ls /)\n"
"['/bin', '/etc', '/home']\n"
"\n"
"@ showcmd echo prefix$(@lines ls /)\n"
"['echo', 'prefix/bin', 'prefix/etc', 'prefix/home']\n"
"\n"
"@ $(@paths ls /)\n"
"[Path('/bin'), Path('/etc'), Path('/home')]\n"
"\n"
"@ $(@path pwd)\n"
"Path('/home/snail')\n"
"\n"
"@ $(@json curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/xonsh/xonsh)"
"['default_branch']\n"
"main\n"
"\n"
"@ $(@jsonl echo '{\"a\":1}\\n{\"b\":2}')\n"
"[{'a': 1}, {'b': 2}]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1430
msgid ""
"See the full list of :ref:`Command Decorators <command-decorators>` "
"(``@yaml``, ``@toml``, ``@xml``, ``@lxml``, etc.) or build the new one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1432
msgid ""
"Using ``DecoratorAlias`` and ``SpecAttrDecoratorAlias`` and callable "
"``output_format`` you can convert subprocess command output into Python "
"object with your own logic:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1435
msgid ""
"from xonsh.procs.specs import SpecAttrDecoratorAlias\n"
"\n"
"aliases['@and'] = SpecAttrDecoratorAlias(\n"
"                    {\"output_format\": lambda lines: ' and "
"'.join([l.strip() for l in lines])},\n"
"                    descr=\"Join lines with 'and'.\", name=\"@and\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1444
msgid "Now you can run:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1446
msgid ""
"@ $(@and ls /)\n"
"'/bin and /etc and /home'"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1454
msgid ""
"Aliasing is a powerful way that xonsh allows you to seamlessly interact to "
"with Python and subprocess. See :doc:`callable_aliases` for the full "
"callable aliases reference including stream capturing, ``env`` overlay, and "
"return values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1459
msgid ""
"If ``FOREIGN_ALIASES_OVERRIDE`` environment variable is False (the default), "
"then foreign shell aliases that try to override xonsh aliases will be "
"ignored. The setting of this environment variable must happen outside if "
"xonsh, i.e. in the process that starts xonsh."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1467
msgid "Prompt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1470
msgid "Up, Down, Tab Completion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1471
msgid ""
"The up and down keys search history matching from the start of the line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1473
msgid ""
"Tab completion is present as well. By default, in Python-mode you are able "
"to complete based on the variable names in the current builtins, globals, "
"and locals, as well as xonsh languages keywords & operator, files & "
"directories, and environment variable names. In subprocess-mode, you "
"additionally complete on the names of executable files on your ``$PATH``, "
"alias keys, and various additional completers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1480
msgid ""
"xonsh also provides a means of modifying the behavior of the tab completer.  "
"More detail is available on the `Tab Completion page <completers.html>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1486
msgid "Customizing the Prompt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1488
msgid ""
"Customizing the prompt by modifying ``$PROMPT``, ``$RIGHT_PROMPT`` or "
"``$BOTTOM_TOOLBAR`` is probably the most common reason for altering an "
"environment variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1491
msgid ""
"Note that the ``$PROMPT`` variable will never be inherited from a parent "
"process (regardless of whether that parent is a foreign shell or an instance "
"of xonsh)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1495
msgid ""
"The ``$PROMPT`` variable can be a string, or it can be a function (of no "
"arguments) that returns a string.  The result can contain keyword arguments, "
"which will be replaced automatically:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1499
msgid ""
"@ $PROMPT = '{user}@{hostname}:{cwd} @ '\n"
"snail@home:~ @ # it works!\n"
"@ $PROMPT = lambda: '{user}@{hostname}:{cwd} @> '\n"
"snail@home:~ @> # so does that!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1506
msgid ""
"See :ref:`customprompt_ref` in the Prompt Toolkit page for the full list of "
"available prompt variables, custom ``PROMPT_FIELDS``, conditional "
"formatting, and virtual environment settings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1512
msgid "Colors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1514
msgid ""
"Xonsh supports colored output in prompts and print functions. Use color "
"keywords like ``{GREEN}`` or ``{BOLD_BLUE}`` and ``{RESET}`` to clear:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1517
msgid ""
"@ print_color('{RED}Error:{RESET} something went wrong')\n"
"@ printx('Success!', 'BOLD_GREEN')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1522
msgid "Colors work in prompts too:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1524
msgid "@ $PROMPT = '{CYAN}{cwd}{RESET} @ '"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1528
msgid ""
"See :doc:`prompt` for the full list of color names, hex colors, and "
"modifiers (bold, italic, underline, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1532
msgid "Executing Commands and Scripts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1533
msgid ""
"When started with the ``-c`` flag and a command, xonsh will execute that "
"command and exit, instead of entering the command loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1537
msgid ""
"When executing commands this way your :doc:`xonsh RC <xonshrc>` files are "
"not applied."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1539
msgid ""
"@ xonsh -c \"echo @(7+3)\"\n"
"10"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1544
msgid ""
"Longer scripts can be run either by specifying a filename containing the "
"script, or by feeding them to xonsh via stdin.  For example, consider the "
"following script, stored in ``test.xsh``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1548
msgid ""
"#!/usr/bin/env xonsh\n"
"\n"
"ls\n"
"\n"
"print('removing files')\n"
"rm `file\\d+.txt`\n"
"\n"
"ls\n"
"\n"
"print('adding files')\n"
"# This is a comment\n"
"for i, x in enumerate(\"xonsh\"):\n"
"    echo @(x) > @(\"file{0}.txt\".format(i))\n"
"\n"
"print($(ls).replace('\\n', ' '))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1567
msgid "This script could be run by piping its contents to xonsh:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1569
msgid ""
"@ cat test.xsh | xonsh\n"
"file0.txt  file1.txt  file2.txt  file3.txt  file4.txt  test_script.sh\n"
"removing files\n"
"test_script.sh\n"
"adding files\n"
"file0.txt file1.txt file2.txt file3.txt file4.txt test_script.sh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1578
msgid "or by invoking xonsh with its filename as an argument:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1580
msgid ""
"@ xonsh test.xsh\n"
"file0.txt  file1.txt  file2.txt  file3.txt  file4.txt  test_script.sh\n"
"removing files\n"
"test_script.sh\n"
"adding files\n"
"file0.txt file1.txt file2.txt file3.txt file4.txt test_script.sh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1589
msgid ""
"xonsh scripts can also accept command line arguments and parameters. These "
"arguments are made available to the script in two different ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1592
msgid "In either mode, as individual variables ``$ARG<n>`` (e.g., ``$ARG1``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1593
msgid "In Python mode only, as a list ``$ARGS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1595
msgid ""
"For example, consider a slight variation of the example script from above "
"that operates on a given argument, rather than on the string ``'xonsh'`` "
"(notice how ``$ARGS`` and ``$ARG1`` are used):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1600
msgid ""
"#!/usr/bin/env xonsh\n"
"\n"
"print($ARGS)\n"
"\n"
"ls\n"
"\n"
"print('removing files')\n"
"rm `file\\d+.txt`\n"
"\n"
"ls\n"
"\n"
"print('adding files')\n"
"This is a comment\n"
"for i, x in enumerate($ARG1):\n"
"    echo @(x) > @(\"file{0}.txt\".format(i))\n"
"\n"
"print($(ls).replace('\\n', ' '))\n"
"print()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1622
msgid ""
"@ xonsh test2.xsh snails\n"
"['test_script.sh', 'snails']\n"
"file0.txt  file1.txt  file2.txt  file3.txt  file4.txt  file5.txt  "
"test_script.sh\n"
"removing files\n"
"test_script.sh\n"
"adding files\n"
"file0.txt file1.txt file2.txt file3.txt file4.txt file5.txt test_script.sh\n"
"\n"
"@ echo @(' '.join($(cat @('file%d.txt' % i)).strip() for i in range(6)))\n"
"s n a i l s"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1635
msgid ""
"Furthermore, you can also toggle the ability to print source code lines with "
"the ``trace on`` and ``trace off`` commands.  This is roughly equivalent to "
"Python's ``python -m trace``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1640
msgid "Error Handling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1642
msgid ""
"Xonsh treats shell commands as first-class code.  When a command fails, you "
"usually want your script to **stop** instead of silently marching past the "
"failure — the way a Python exception would — but you also want the "
"flexibility of ``&&``/``||`` short-circuit logic that the shell is built "
"around."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1648
msgid ""
"By default, execution stops as soon as a command **chain** ends in a failing "
"command.  A chain is any group of commands whose result is determined "
"together — a pipe, a logical ``&&``/``||`` expression, or a bare single "
"command.  It is the *final* result of the chain that decides whether "
"execution continues; individual commands that are explicitly rescued by ``||"
"`` are not fatal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1655
msgid "A couple of examples:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1657
msgid ""
"@ echo hi | grep x                  # pipe chain — grep didn't match → "
"raise\n"
"@ ls nofile && echo never           # && chain — ls failed → raise, echo "
"skipped\n"
"@ ls nofile || echo rescued         # || chain — rescued by echo, no raise\n"
"rescued\n"
"@ (echo 1 && ls /etc) || echo fb    # nested — inner chain succeeded, no "
"raise"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1665
msgid ""
"The **only** subprocess form that does not stop execution on failure is the "
"full-capture operator ``!(...)``: it returns a ``CommandPipeline`` object "
"and leaves error handling entirely up to you.  This is the idiomatic way to "
"inspect a command's result without triggering an exception:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1671
msgid ""
"@ if !(ls nofile):\n"
"      print(\"found\")\n"
"  else:\n"
"      print(\"absent\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1678
msgid ""
"See :ref:`error_handling` for the full rules, including ``@error_raise``/"
"``@error_ignore`` decorators, the environment variables that tune this "
"behavior, and how the interactive prompt displays (or hides) the resulting "
"exception."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1684
msgid "Importing Xonsh (``*.xsh``)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1685
msgid ""
"You can import xonsh source files with the ``*.xsh`` file extension using "
"the normal Python syntax:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1688
msgid "from mine import *"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1693
msgid "Compile, Evaluate, & Execute"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1694
msgid ""
"Xonsh provides built-in hooks to compile, evaluate, and execute strings of "
"xonsh code.  To prevent this functionality from having serious name "
"collisions with the Python built-in ``compile()``, ``eval()``, and "
"``exec()`` functions, the xonsh equivalents all append an 'x'.  So for xonsh "
"code you want to use the ``compilex()``, ``evalx()``, and ``execx()`` "
"functions. If you don't know what these do, you probably don't need them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1703
msgid "Help & Superhelp with ``?`` & ``??``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1704
msgid ""
"Xonsh allows you to inspect objects with question marks. A single question "
"mark (``?``) is used to display the normal level of help. Double question "
"marks (``??``) are used to display a higher level of help, called superhelp. "
"Superhelp usually includes source code if the object was written in pure "
"Python."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorial.rst:1710
msgid ""
"@ int?\n"
"Convert a number or string to an integer, or return 0 if no arguments\n"
"are given.  If x is a number, return x.__int__().  For floating point\n"
"numbers, this truncates towards zero.\n"
"\n"
"@ @.imp.json.loads??\n"
"def loads(s, *, cls=None, object_hook=None, parse_float=None,\n"
"   parse_int=None, parse_constant=None, object_pairs_hook=None, **kw):\n"
